G-D — non-blocking pair: both closed.** N1 (anchors): all eight re-derived\n99|against the candidate tree and exact — `manifest.go` 1696/1699 (guards),\n100|1741/1777/2099/2102 (naming sites), `terminalbackend.go` 646 (descriptor guard),\n101|163 (census note). N2 (`R-tb-checktuple-order`): now filed `class: \"ordering\"`\n102|in `TASK-260906-2okwyf_battery-r3.json`, and its mutant body is a genuine move\n103|of the guard past the version arms, not a narrowing.\n104|\n105|**G-E — battery and provenance: PASS.** 6 applied / 6 killed / 0 survived on a\n106|production-derived denominator; classes separate (`narrowing` 1, `arm-deletion`\n107|2, `ordering` 1, `census-only` 1, `audit-only` 1); `NOT_APPLIED` and\n108|`COMPILE_FAIL` are distinct control rows outside the denominator; masks are\n109|non-empty and explicit (`census_run` 15 tests, `behav_run` 12 tests → 273 ran,\n110|`audit_run` \"\" → full package 695 ran); mutant bodies (old/new) are in\n111|`TASK-260906-2okwyf_mutants-r3.json` and I read them rather than the labels —\n112|the narrowing row is a real narrowing (guard retained, wrapped in\n113|`len > maxIDBytes`), and P9 reproduced its verdict. Candidate tree equals the\n114|record's, `ls-tree` confirms all three new files, `HEAD` is still `114a056` with\n115|no commit of the producer's own.\n116|\n117|---\n118|\n119|## 3. AC coverage — 4 of 4 rows driven by this run\n120|\n121|| AC row | production call site | how this run drove it |\n122||---|---|---|\n123|| two six-provider literals equal directly, either side alone reddens, SPEC-independent | `provider.Builtins()` / `provhost.profileProviders` | P4/P5/P6 — reorder, rename, removal, each on one side alone, each killed by `TestBuiltinsEqualProfileProviders` under `-run` isolation. The test reads no SPEC.md. |\n124|| §6.5 vs §7.1 asymmetry cited at both sites with the spec clause | `terminalbackend.DigestFile` (`terminalbackend.go:681-693`), `provider.trustCandidate` (`provider.go:393-401`) | verified verbatim against `agent-session-manager-spec@28bf96d7` — §6.5 \"Each external-trust entry contains exactly backend ID, absolute executable path, executable digest, and `enabled`\" (no owner member) and §7.1 \"the target MUST be a regular file owned by the operator or an administrator-approved identity\". Both citations are exact and cross-reference each other. |\n125|| `ParseID` prints no unbounded refused input | `terminalbackend.ParseID` + every ID entry | 5-vector production drive through `Reconcile` (table §1); the one echo is the declared reserved-namespace bound, ≤128B and grammar-conforming. |\n126|| every closed nit has a fail-before test; every open nit a stated bound | `Reconcile` entry, `decodeValidatedProbe`, `parseMajor` | P9 (Reconcile narrowing), P10 (RequireCapability ordering — the single-decode refactor's behavioural claim reproduces), P8 + `DecodeResponse` classification read (parseMajor left open with a bound whose *justification* I verified empirically, not just read). |\n127|\n128|The named-rune correction in `digit_guard_census_test.go` is also verified by\n129|execution: P7 reproduces the blind spot the corrected sentence now claims (the\n130|previous wording asserted the opposite), and an independent grep finds 0\n131|digit-valued named constants in either package.\n132|\n133|---\n134|\n135|## 4. Findings\n136|\n137|### F3 — BLOCKING: an undeclared scratch file is in the candidate and would land in `main`\n138|\n139|`probe_f2_main.go.bak` — 80 lines, `package main`, the developer's F2 reproduction\n140|probe — sits at the **repository root** of the candidate tree. It is untracked,\n141|not covered by `.gitignore` (`git check-ignore` exits 1), present in\n142|`git ls-tree 34b88fb9…`, and carried in\n143|`TASK-260906-2okwyf_change-request_rev3.patch` as one of the 13 changed paths.\n144|\n145|Failure scenario: `accept_cr` binds this exact tree; the integration transaction\n146|lands it, and an 80-line scratch `package main` at the root of a spec-conformant\n147|Go repository enters `main`'s permanent history. The repository instructions put\n148|validation artifacts under `.temp/<TASK-ID>/`; this is outside both that path\n149|and the declared story boundary.\n150|\n151|This is not only hygiene. `TASK-260906-2okwyf_outcome-r3.md` §\"Round-3 scope\"\n152|states the scope as **\"3 code/test paths + LOGBOOK\"** — the candidate has four\n153|non-LOGBOOK paths in this round. The same document reports the tree OID as\n154|\"verified by listing the tree after every artifact is written\"; the listing was\n155|performed but its contents were evidently not read back against the declared\n156|scope, so the verification step reported agreement it had not checked. That is\n157|the reported-facts class this task has been iterating on, arriving at the\n158|provenance step itself.\n159|\n160|**Fix:** delete `probe_f2_main.go.bak` (or move it under\n161|`.temp/TASK-260906-2okwyf/`), re-derive the candidate tree OID, and make the\n162|outcome's scope statement enumerate exactly the paths the Change Request carries.\n163|No test change is needed; the repair is verifiable by `git ls-tree <new OID>`\n164|containing no root-level `.bak` and by the scope list matching the CR's changed\n165|paths one-for-one.\n166|\n167|### N1 — non-blocking: the census site filter is source-text-keyed and blind to a multi-line literal\n168|\n169|`deriveBackendIDSites` selects the denominator with\n170|`strings.Contains(lines[arm.line-1], \"BackendID:\")`\n171|(`backend_id_entry_census_test.go:337`). Its own comment claims \"a new BackendID\n172|arm is derived without anyone remembering this file\". P3 disproves that for one\n173|spelling: a new arm written as a multi-line composite literal keeps `BackendID:`\n174|off the derived arm's line, so the site never enters the denominator, no pair is\n175|demanded, and `TestBackendIDEntryCensus` stays green at 41/41 while an\n176|unvalidated `probe.ProbeID` echoes.\n177|\n178|Why non-blocking: measured 0 live instances (0 multi-line `&Error{` constructions\n179|in either package's production source), and the same plant is caught closed by\n180|four sibling instruments (`TestDerivedSiteLinesAreExactlyRowed`,\n181|`TestDeclaredRefusalArmsAreAllDerived`, `TestDerivedArmsAreAllWitnessed`,\n182|`TestDerivedRefusalArmsAreAllDeclared`), so an unregistered arm cannot slip in.\n183|The gap is that a *registered* multi-line arm would satisfy those and still miss\n184|the entry census. Either key the filter on the composite literal's AST keys\n185|rather than the line text, or state the single-line spelling as a bound.\n186|\n187|### N2 — non-blocking: `deriveExportedEntries` fails **open** on a bare-name collision\n188|\n189|The entry derivation dedupes by bare function name and states in-comment \"Every\n190|bare name is unique in this package: no two exported methods share one\". Nothing\n191|enforces it. P2: an exported method `(*Registry).Reconcile` reaching the\n192|unvalidated `checkProbeIdentity` arms is absorbed by `seen[name]` and the census\n193|stays green — the *same collapse-two-entry-paths-into-one-row* shape the round-2\n194|finding was about, one level up in the instrument. Measured today: 59 distinct\n195|exported bare names, **0 collisions**, so the assumption holds and there is no\n196|live hole. A three-line `t.Fatalf` on a duplicate bare name converts it from an\n197|unenforced comment into a closed gate.\n198|\n199|### N3 — non-blocking: the 44 free-entry reasons are unchecked prose\n200|\n201|`checkBackendIDMapping` requires a free entry to carry a non-empty `reason` and\n202|to be a derived entry; nothing checks the reason's content. A free entry that\n203|*reaches* a paired site with unvalidated input would pass (the site already has a\n204|pair through another entry). I checked this myself with a `go/ast` call-graph\n205|sweep: **0 of 44** free entries reach a BackendID-naming refusal, so the list is\n206|sound today. Recording it as a stated bound, or deriving \"constructs no BackendID\n207|refusal\" from the call graph, would close it.\n208|\n209|### N4 — non-blocking: outcome §2 entry arithmetic is off by one\n210|\n211|\"every exported entry (58 derived: 10 pair entries + 5 hostile-only + 43 free\n212|with reasons)\". The instrument derives **59**, and `backendIDFreeEntries` has\n213|**44** rows (10 + 5 + 44 = 59). The instrument is right; the document's summary\n214|is not. Also `LOGBOOK.md` line for round 2 says \"review rev3 CHANGES REQUESTED\"\n215|where it means rev 1.\n216|\n217|---\n218|\n219|## 5. Summary\n220|\n221|The substantive work is sound and I could not defeat it. The rev-1/rev-2 echo\n222|class is closed at the entry dimension and measured at its edge, not merely its\n223|existence; the cross-package agreement pin kills a one-sided reorder, rename and\n224|removal; both spec citations are verbatim-accurate against the pinned spec\n225|commit; the two stated bounds (`parseMajor` looseness, the named-rune blind spot)\n226|had their *justifications* reproduced by execution rather than accepted as prose;\n227|and the battery is honest at the level of mutant bodies. Four of four AC rows are\n228|driven through production entry points by this run.\n229|\n230|The single blocking defect is F3: an undeclared 80-line scratch file inside the\n231|accepted tree, contradicting the outcome's own scope statement, which `accept_cr`\n232|would bind and integration would land in `main`. It is a one-file deletion plus a\n233|corrected scope line, and the reviewer cannot make it — this run is read-only."}}}
